WEBVTT

1
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.500
Greetings, my friend. We are
all interested in the future,

2
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.199
...for that is where you and I
are going to spend the rest of our lives.

3
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.000
And remember my friend, future events such
as these will affect you in the future.

4
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.837
You are interested in the...

5
00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.300
...unknown, the mysterious,
the unexplainable.

6
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.500
That is why you are here.
And now, for the first time,

7
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.500
...we are bringing to you the full story
of what happened on that fateful day.

8
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.000
We are giving you all the evidence, based
only on the secret testimonies...

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.799
...of the miserable souls who survived...

10
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.800
...this terrifying ordeal. The
incidents, the places,

11
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.783
...my friend we cannot keep
this a secret any longer.

12
00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:11.800
Let us punish the guilty,

13
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:17.399
...let us reward the innocent.
My friend,

14
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.599
...can your heart stand the shocking facts
about grave robbers...

15
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.600
...from outer space?

16
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.200
Plan 9 From Outer Space

17
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.600
All of us on this earth, know that
there is a time to live,

18
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.900
...and that there is a time to die,
yet death is always a shock...

19
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.600
...to those left behind.

20
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.865
It is even more of a shock, when death,

21
00:02:59.965 --> 00:03:04.215
...the proud brother, comes
suddenly without warning.

22
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.399
Just at sundown, a small group,

23
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.300
...gathered in silent prayer around
the newly-opened grave...

24
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.500
...of the beloved wife of an elderly man.
Sundown of the day,

25
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.700
...yet also the sundown of
the old man's heart,

26
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.200
...for the shadows of grief
clouded his very reason.

27
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.460
The funeral over, the saddened
group left the graveside.

28
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.300
It was when the gravediggers
started their task...

29
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.400
...that strange things began to take place.

30
00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.599
Fifteen to four.

31
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.400
Yup, right on schedule. There's the
ol' San Fernando Valley...

32
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.099
- ...out there now.
- You better radio in...

33
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.500
- ...for landing instructions, Danny.
- Right, Jeff.

34
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.680
Burbank Tower, this is American
Flight 812, over.

35
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.193
Wouldn't surprise...

36
00:04:34.293 --> 00:04:37.400
...me any if he's asleep
this time of the morning.

37
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.000
American Flight 812, this is Burbank Tower.
If I were asleep...

38
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.102
...you'd never get on the ground.

39
00:04:43.202 --> 00:04:46.200
Your case maybe you'd be
up there for good. Over.

40
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.655
You got me that time, Mac.
This American Flight 812 requesting...

41
00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.800
Burbank Tower to American
Flight 812, over.

42
00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:03.900
Burbank Tower to American
Flight 812, over.

43
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.900
- Holy mackeral.
- Burbank Tower to American Flight 812,

44
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.600
...are you in trouble?

45
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.900
- Trouble?
- Take a look for yourself.

46
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.600
- What in the world...
- That's nothing from this world.

47
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.700
Burbank Tower to American Flight 812,
are you in trouble? Are you in trouble?

48
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.932
Mayday, mayday. Stand
by, Burbank Tower.

49
00:05:27.032 --> 00:05:29.000
Do you suppose the passengers saw it?

50
00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.600
I doubt it. Most of them are asleep.
But it was quite a jolt, Jeff. I'll check.

51
00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.000
Good. We'll get them ready for landing.
Keep it quiet until we get instructions.

52
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.100
Right.

53
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.705
American Flight 812 reporting
to Burbank Tower, over.

54
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.300
- D'you hear anything?
- I thought I did.

55
00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:10.144
Don't like hearin' noises.

56
00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:11.600
'Specially when there
ain't s'posed to be any.

57
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.299
Yeah, sorta spooky-like.

58
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.600
- Maybe we're gettin' old.
- Whatever it is it's gone now.

59
00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.200
That's the best thing for us too. Gone.

60
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.300
Yeah, let's go.

61
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.999
The grief of his wife's death...

62
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.000
...became greater and greater agony.

63
00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.420
The home they had so
long shared together,

64
00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.300
...became a tomb.

65
00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.400
A sweet memory of her joyous living.

66
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:27.015
The sky to which she had once looked,
was now only a covering for her dead body.

67
00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:46.400
The ever-beautiful flowers she had
planted with her own hands,

68
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.105
...became nothing more than the
lost roses of her cheeks.

69
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.300
Confused by his great loss, the old man...

70
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.400
...left that home,
never to return again.

71
00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.390
At the funeral of the old man,
unknown to his mourners,

72
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.000
...his dead wife was watching.

73
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.699
- First his wife, then he.
- Tragic.

74
00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.900
Tell me something. Why was
his wife buried in the ground,

75
00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:36.900
- ...and he sealed in a crypt?
- Something to do with family tradition.

76
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.199
A superstition of some sort.

77
00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.450
Well, it's getting' dark.
We'd best be on our way.

78
00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.530
Then, as two of his mourners
left his final resting place.

79
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.800
Minutes later, the police,
lead by Inspector Daniel Clay,

80
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.099
...arrived at the scene.

81
00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.299
- Who found him?
- The man and girl.

82
00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:40.300
- Medical, uh, examiner been 'round yet?
- Just left.

83
00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.300
The morgue wagon oughta
be along most any time.

84
00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.400
You get their statement?

85
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:48.000
Yeah, much as we could.
They're pretty scared.

86
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:52.300
Finding a mess like this
oughta make anyone frightened.

87
00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:57.300
Have one of the boys take the guy
and the girl back to town. You take charge.

88
00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.300
Okay Inspector. What're you gonna do?

89
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.800
- Look around a little.
- Pretty dark out there.

90
00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:04.467
Once you get beyond the range of
those lights you won't be...

91
00:10:04.567 --> 00:10:06.000
...able to see your hand
in front of your face.

92
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.100
I will get one of the
flashlights from the patrol car.

93
00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.200
- You be careful Clay.
- I'm a big boy now, Johnny.

94
00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:12.400
Looks like a bobcat tore into them.

95
00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.500
Yeah.

96
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:19.899
Say Lieutenant,

97
00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.980
- ...d'you get that funny odour?
- How could I miss it?

98
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.560
Oh, that'll be the morgue wagon now.

99
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.200
- That's the fifth siren in the last hour.
- Oh, something's happened down at...

100
00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:35.017
...the cemetery. A lot of
police cars and lights.

101
00:11:35.117 --> 00:11:37.100
I stopped but I didn't see anything.

102
00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.065
Oh well, whatever it is, the morning paper
will carry the whole story.

103
00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.900
You seem to still be up there somewhere.

104
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.399
Maybe I am.

105
00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:57.820
I don't think I've ever seen you
in this mood before.

106
00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:03.860
I guess it's because I've never been
in this mood before.

107
00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.899
Something about your flight?

108
00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.799
Yeah.

109
00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.800
What happened, Jeff?

110
00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.399
- I saw a flying saucer.
- A saucer?

111
00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:30.330
- You mean the kind from up there?
- Yeah, or its counterpart.

112
00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:34.500
I was shaped like a huge cigar.
Danny saw it too.

113
00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.858
When it passed over,
the whole compartment...

114
00:12:36.958 --> 00:12:38.600
...lighted up with a blinding glare.

115
00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.783
Then there was a tremendous
wind that practically...

116
00:12:40.883 --> 00:12:41.900
...knocked us off our course.

117
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.210
Well did you report it?

118
00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:46.000
Yeah, radioed in immediately
and they said well...

119
00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:47.558
...keep it quiet until you land.

120
00:12:47.658 --> 00:12:50.200
Then as soon as we landed,
big army brass grabbed us...

121
00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.300
...and made us swear to
secrecy about the whole thing.

122
00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:57.664
Oh, it burns me up. These things
have been seen for years.

123
00:12:57.764 --> 00:12:59.200
They're here, it's a fact.

124
00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:00.521
And the public oughta know about it.

125
00:13:00.621 --> 00:13:02.300
There must be something
more you can do about it.

126
00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:07.000
Oh no there isn't.
Oh, but what's the point of making a fuss.

127
00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.215
Last night I saw a flying object that...

128
00:13:09.315 --> 00:13:11.700
...couldn't possibly have
been from this planet.

129
00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:16.000
But I can't say a word.
I'm muzzled by army brass.

130
00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.075
I can't even admit I saw the thing.

131
00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.850
Sounds like Clay's in trouble.

132
00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.400
Bet that apparition we saw had
something to do with it.

133
00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.500
Come on.

134
00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:02.000
- Is he dead?
- Yeah.

135
00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:06.300
He's messed up as bad as
those two back there.

136
00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.900
S'pose that saucer or whatever it was
had something to do with this?

137
00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.900
Your guess is as good as mine, Larry.

138
00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:16.700
But one thing's sure.
Inspector Clay is dead...

139
00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:20.800
...murdered...and somebody's responsible.

140
00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.500
You're in charge now, Lieutenant.

141
00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:27.599
- Yeah, guess I am. Kelton.
- Yes Sir?

142
00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:29.372
Get back up to the car
and get on the radio.

143
00:16:29.472 --> 00:16:31.700
Tell the coroner he's gotta
make another trip out here.

144
00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.600
Well how 'bout the lab boys?

145
00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:35.999
Well who do you think we
left back up the car,

146
00:16:36.001 --> 00:16:39.955
...boy scouts? Come on, Larry.

147
00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:50.500
Greater love hath no man, than to
lay down his life for another.

148
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.000
It is always difficult
to have last words...

149
00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:59.500
...over the grave of a friend.
And Inspector Daniel Clay was a friend.

150
00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:03.800
A dear friend to me and to all of us.

151
00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.400
The bell has rung upon his great career.

152
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Now we lay him to rest. A rest
well deserved, but so premature.

153
00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.100
People turning south from the freeway
were startled when they saw...

154
00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:36.280
...flying saucers high
over Hollywood Boulevard.

155
00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:55.640
A woman, startled by the sight in the
sky, telephones the police.

156
00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.800
There comes a time in each man's life,

157
00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:24.900
...when he can't even believe his own eyes.

158
00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.400
Saucers seen over Hollywood.

159
00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.800
Flying saucers seen over Washington D.C.

160
00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.700
The army convoy moved into the field.

161
00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:01.099
Rockets were quickly set up.

162
00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.700
Colonel Tom Edwards, in charge
of saucer field activities,

163
00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.900
...was to make the greatest
decision of his career.

164
00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:13.355
He made that decision. Colonel
Edwards gave the signal to fire.

165
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.800
Then as swiftly as they had come,
they were gone.

166
00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:26.340
Even to the piercing eye of radar
and the speeding jet fighters.

167
00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:36.300
- Quite a sight, wasn't it Sir.
- A sight I'd rather not be seeing.

168
00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.300
Are you worried about them Sir?

169
00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.300
Well, they must have a
reason for their visits.

170
00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:43.920
Visits? Well that would indicate visitors.

171
00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:48.185
Are big guns the usual
way of welcoming visitors?

172
00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:51.599
We haven't always fired at them.

173
00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:55.800
For a time we tried to contact
them by radio, but no response.

174
00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:59.841
Then they attacked a town.
A small town, I'll admit.

175
00:20:59.941 --> 00:21:02.500
But nevertheless a town of people.

176
00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:06.200
- People who died.
- I never heard about that Sir.

177
00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:11.485
Well, it was covered up by the
higher echelon. Take any fire,

178
00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:15.199
...any earthquake, any major disaster,

179
00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.260
...then wonder.
Flying saucers, Captain,

180
00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.899
...are still a rumour.

181
00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.900
Officially.

182
00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.300
Looks like we beat them off again Sir.

183
00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:32.300
What do they want... where are they
from... where are they going...

184
00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.799
They, Sir? Who?

185
00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.525
Oh, this is a training manoeuver, Sir.

186
00:21:37.625 --> 00:21:41.300
We only did a little practice
firing at the clouds.

187
00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.140
Yeah. I wonder what
their next move will be.

188
00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:01.465
What will their next move be?

189
00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:09.300
Your space commander
has returned from Earth.

190
00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.400
Send him in.

191
00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.000
You have your report?

192
00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.900
We had to pull in here to
Space Station 7 for regeneration.

193
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:27.100
We're returning to the planet Earth
immediately thereafter.

194
00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:31.000
- What progress has been made?
- We contacted government officials.

195
00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:33.395
They refuse our existence.

196
00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:36.500
What plan will you follow now?

197
00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:40.800
Plan 9. It's been absolutely impossible
to work through these Earth creatures.

198
00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.365
Their soul is too controlled.

199
00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:48.900
Plan 9... ah yes. Plan 9 deals...

200
00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:51.400
...with the resurrection of the dead.

201
00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:54.570
Long-distance electrodes
shot into the pinion...

202
00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:56.700
...pituitary glands of recent dead.

203
00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.700
- Have you attempted any of this plan as yet?
- Yes, Excellency.

204
00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:02.299
How successful has it been?

205
00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:07.200
We have risen two so far.
We shall be just as successful on more.

206
00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:11.400
The living... they have no
suspicion of your movements?

207
00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:13.500
We had to dispose of one policeman.

208
00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.941
However, none of those
risen have been seen.

209
00:23:16.041 --> 00:23:18.600
At least, not by anyone
who still remains alive.

210
00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:23.630
It's too bad it must be handled
this way, but it must.

211
00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:27.700
Those who we take from the grave will
lead the way for our other operations.

212
00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:31.699
- Yes, Excellency.
- Continue on. Report to me...

213
00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.700
...in two Earth days.

214
00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:39.900
I feared His Excellency wouldn't
take our report this well.

215
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.888
Well had he been dealing
with our own people...

216
00:23:41.988 --> 00:23:44.000
...his reaction would have
been completely different.

217
00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.600
He understands the difficulties
of the Earth race.

218
00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:50.700
What do you think will be the next obstacle
the Earth people will put in our way?

219
00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:55.560
Well, as long as they can
think, we'll have our problems.

220
00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:59.122
But those whom we're using
cannot think. They are the dead.

221
00:23:59.222 --> 00:24:02.200
Brought to a simulated life
by our electrode guns.

222
00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:05.279
You know, it's an
interesting thing when you...

223
00:24:05.379 --> 00:24:08.100
...consider... the Earth
people, who can think,

224
00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:12.280
...are so frightened by those
who cannot: The dead.

225
00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.215
Well our ship should be
regenerated. We better get started.

226
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:16.400
I still think you oughta go in town and
stay with your mother until I get back.

227
00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:20.000
This is our home and nothing's
going to take me from it.

228
00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:24.500
Besides, most men try and keep their
wives from going home to Momma.

229
00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.400
- That's not the point.
- That's all the point there's going to be.

230
00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.050
Now toddle off and fly your
flying machine, Darling.

231
00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:32.400
But if you see any more...

232
00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:37.600
...flying saucers, will you tell
them to pick another house to buzz?

233
00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:41.499
Be careful.
Don't worry about me.

234
00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:45.400
Oh you're the only thing I do worry about.
Oh forget about the flying saucers.

235
00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:52.000
They're up there. But there's
something in that cemetery,

236
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:55.299
...and that's too close for comfort.

237
00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:59.100
The saucers are up there.
And the cemetery's out there.

238
00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:05.800
But I'll be locked up in there.
Now off to your wild blue yonders.

239
00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.100
You promise you'll lock
the doors immediately?

240
00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:12.100
I promise. Besides, I'll be in
bed before half an hour is gone,

241
00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:15.499
- ...with your pillow beside me.
- My pillow?

242
00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:20.500
Well, I have to have something to
keep me company while you're away.

243
00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:25.310
Sometimes in the night when
it does get a little lonely,

244
00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:31.020
...I reach over and touch it, then it
doesn't seem so lonely anymore.

245
00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.400
A crazy kid.

246
00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:44.700
I do love you, Darlin'.

247
00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:01.900
- See you Thursday.
- Goodbye, Honey.

248
00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.700
You know I'm not leaving here
until you're locked safely inside.

249
00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.800
All right, Darling.

250
00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:25.600
If you're especially nice I
may even lock the side door.

251
00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:29.700
And be sure you keep the yard lights on.

252
00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:53.999
You're mighty silent this trip, Jeff.

253
00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:55.900
You haven't spoken ten
words since takeoff.

254
00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:59.199
I guess I'm preoccupied, Danny.

255
00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:02.200
We've got thirty-three passengers back
there that have time to be preoccupied.

256
00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:05.700
Flying this flybird doesn't
give you that opportunity.

257
00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.899
I guess you're right, Danny.

258
00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:12.099
- Paula?
- Yeah.

259
00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:15.200
- There's nothing wrong between you two?
- Oh no, nothing like that.

260
00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:17.035
Just that I'm worried, she
being there alone and...

261
00:28:17.135 --> 00:28:18.800
...those strange things
flying over the house and...

262
00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:23.600
...those incidents in the graveyard the
past few days. It's just got me worried.

263
00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:26.257
Well, I haven't figured
out those crazy skybirds...

264
00:28:26.357 --> 00:28:28.300
...yet but I give you fifty to one odds...

265
00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:30.700
...the police have figured out
that cemetery thing by now.

266
00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:31.800
I hope so.

267
00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:35.000
If you're really that worried Jeff
why don't you radio in and find out?

268
00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:37.219
Mac should be on duty at the field by now.

269
00:28:37.319 --> 00:28:39.800
He could call Paula and
relay the message to you.

270
00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:40.900
Hi Edith.

271
00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.400
Hi Silents. I haven't heard a word from this
end of the plane since we left the field.

272
00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:47.400
Jeff's been giving
himself and me a study in silence.

273
00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:50.600
- You boys are feudin'?
- Oh no Edie, nothing like that.

274
00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:53.300
Hey Edie, how about you and me
bowling it up in Albuquerque?

275
00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.800
Albuquerque? Have you read
that flight schedule Boy?

276
00:28:55.900 --> 00:28:56.900
What about it?

277
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:01.100
We land in Albuquerque at 4 am.
That's strictly a nine o'clock town.

278
00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:03.400
Well I know a friend that'll help us...

279
00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:06.800
- Let's have a problem first, huh Danny.
- Ah he's worried about Paula.

280
00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:09.694
I read about that cemetery business.

281
00:29:09.794 --> 00:29:12.500
I tried to get you kids to not
buy too near one of those things.

282
00:29:12.901 --> 00:29:14.800
We get there soon enough as it is.

283
00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:16.500
He thought it'd be
quiet and peaceful there.

284
00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:21.020
No doubt about that.
It's quiet alright, like a tomb.

285
00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:23.400
I'm sorry Jeff, that was a bad joke.

286
00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:25.592
Say, I almost forgot
what I came in here for.

287
00:29:25.692 --> 00:29:26.900
How's the coffee situation?

288
00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:30.000
- That's for me.
- That sure wouldn't hurt a thing, Edie.

289
00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:34.500
Okay, I'll be right back. And say
Jeff, make that call to your wife.

290
00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:37.600
Huh, not only will she throw cold water
on my Albuquerque plan but now she's...

291
00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:38.700
...repeating herself.

292
00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:44.750
- How 'bout that Albuquerque bowl?
- I can't resist your charm, Danny Boy.

293
00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:54.000
Residents near the cemetery paid little
attention to the blast of thunder...

294
00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:56.799
...and the flash of lightning.

295
00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:02.240
But from the blast, arose the
moving figure of the dead old man.

296
00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.000
Hello? Who?

297
00:30:34.100 --> 00:30:38.100
Mac? Well, hi Mac.

298
00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.499
Sure I'm all right.

299
00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.500
I just fell asleep.

300
00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:48.099
Tell Jeff I'm all right.

301
00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:52.100
Okay Mac. Thanks for calling. Goodnight.

302
00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:19.700
Mrs. Trent. Mrs. Trent. What's wrong?

303
00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:36.155
They'll be at the hatch in a moment.
You can open it now, Tanna.

304
00:36:55.900 --> 00:37:01.680
Turn off the electrodes quickly.
They can't tell us from anyone else.

305
00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:27.600
It's tough to find something when you
don't know what you're looking for.

306
00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:29.468
I don't think the Lieutenant does either.

307
00:37:29.568 --> 00:37:32.000
Then What're doing out here?
I was off duty an hour ago.

308
00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:38.815
Ah don't ask me any questions.
I'm just a hard-hand just like you.

309
00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:46.000
What do you suppose that noise was?

310
00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:48.033
Whatever it was it's no
more strange than the...

311
00:37:48.133 --> 00:37:49.900
...other things happening
around this cemetery.

312
00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.300
Spirits like Old Farmer
Caulder talked about.

313
00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:54.400
Heh. Maybe.

314
00:37:54.500 --> 00:37:57.500
The only spirits he saw tonight
were those I smelled on his breath.

315
00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.762
Well don't forget Mrs. Trent
claims to have seen them too.

316
00:37:59.862 --> 00:38:01.300
She didn't have anything on her breath.

317
00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:02.480
She was hysterical.

318
00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.800
Well true, she was frightened,
and in a state of shock.

319
00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:10.900
But, don't forget that torn
nightgown and the scratched feet.

320
00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.320
Yeah I hadn't thought of that.

321
00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:17.400
I guess that's why you're
a detective lieutenant...

322
00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:20.000
...and I'm still a uniformed cop.

323
00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:22.400
Sometimes it's only the breaks,
Larry. In the meantime let's get...

324
00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:25.300
- Lieutenant, Lieutenant. Did you hear that?
- How could we help it?

325
00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:27.200
- It sure was strange.
- Know what it was?

326
00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:28.300
No more than you do.

327
00:38:28.400 --> 00:38:30.600
If it weren't for orders I'd
get out of here right now.

328
00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:31.296
It was a saucer.

329
00:38:31.396 --> 00:38:33.000
A flying saucer? What makes you say that?

330
00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:35.600
You remember the noise we
heard the other night?

331
00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.100
We were knocked to the
ground, how could I forget?

332
00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:41.900
Exactly, but you're not
remembering that sound.

333
00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:43.666
There you're wrong, Lieutenant.

334
00:38:43.766 --> 00:38:47.700
I'm with a fact the sound is similar,
but what about the blinding light?

335
00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:51.600
Well haven't you heard?
Many times a saucer hasn't had a glow,

336
00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:56.800
- ...or a light of any kind for that matter.
- That proves it. What next Lieutenant?

337
00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:58.670
Maybe this doesn't mean much,

338
00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:03.300
...but uh Jamie and me found a grave
that looks like it's been busted into.

339
00:39:03.400 --> 00:39:04.400
- What? Where?
- Why... uh... why...

340
00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:07.900
Come on man out with it, we
haven't got all day to waste.

341
00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:13.525
- Uh, just over there beyond the crypt.
- All right, show us the way.

342
00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:17.900
Look, here it is Lieutenant.

343
00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:23.000
Ah it's been broken into all right.

344
00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:28.400
Strange. If someone had broken in, the
dirt should be piled up here somewhere.

345
00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:30.939
It looks like it's fallen
in into the grave.

346
00:39:31.039 --> 00:39:34.100
Larry you'll be out of that
uniform before you know it.

347
00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:36.991
Do we have the right to
look down there Lieutenant?

348
00:39:37.091 --> 00:39:38.100
Ah, technically no.

349
00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.271
Well, this spot looks familiar, though.

350
00:39:40.371 --> 00:39:44.400
We shouldn't investigate any further
without the permission of next of kin.

351
00:39:44.500 --> 00:39:45.700
Let's go get it.

352
00:39:45.800 --> 00:39:46.800
How?

353
00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:50.000
I see what you mean, the
gravestone's down there.

354
00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:52.500
Well, let's go down and
find out who's grave it is.

355
00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:56.399
- How?
- By going down and finding out.

356
00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:00.400
- Are you sure you mean that Lieutenant?
- If I didn't mean it I wouldn't have said it.

357
00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:01.500
Scared?

358
00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:04.900
Well, why do I always get hooked
up with these spook details?

359
00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:08.499
Monsters, graves, bodies....

360
00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:10.500
...oooh all right.

361
00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:22.199
Casket's here, but nobody's in it.

362
00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:25.999
Can you read the name on the casket?

363
00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:29.299
It's too dark. Give me a flashlight.

364
00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:34.230
- How 'bout a match?
- We sure could try it. Let me have them.

365
00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:47.400
It's Inspector Clay's grave.
Be he ain't in it.

366
00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:57.635
But meanwhile, in the
Pentagon, in Washington DC...

367
00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:06.899
Try G2.

368
00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:09.499
Come in.

369
00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:13.500
Yes of course, I'll keep in touch.

370
00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:20.899
Come in, Colonel Edwards. Close
the door. At ease, Colonel.

371
00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:24.600
- Thank you, Sir.
- Sit down.

372
00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:33.000
I understand, Colonel, you've been on
tap for many of our saucer attacks.

373
00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:36.699
I'm in charge of field operations, Sir.

374
00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:40.900
You believe there are such things
as flying saucers, Colonel?

375
00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:43.199
- Yes sir.
- You've seen them?

376
00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:44.900
Yes sir.

377
00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:47.605
You realize there's a
government directive...

378
00:41:47.705 --> 00:41:50.900
...stating that there is no
such thing as a flying saucer?

379
00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:52.500
Yes sir.

380
00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:55.800
Do you stand by your statement
that you've seen flying saucers?

381
00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:59.299
Well, uh...

382
00:41:59.300 --> 00:42:02.099
...yes sir.

383
00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:07.100
This could mean a court Marshall.
Admitting this against direct orders.

384
00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:10.599
General Roberts, may I speak freely?

385
00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:11.600
You may.

386
00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:13.500
How could I hope to
hold down my command...

387
00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:17.765
...if I didn't believe in
what I saw and shot at?

388
00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:23.600
- I, uh, like you Colonel.
- Thank you, Sir.

389
00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:28.400
There are flying saucers.

390
00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:34.700
There's no doubt they are in our skies.
They've been there for some time.

391
00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:36.300
What're we going to do about them?

392
00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:40.099
- Who knows?
- Then, uh, they really are there?

393
00:42:40.100 --> 00:42:44.100
- I thought you were convinced of that.
- I am.

394
00:42:44.300 --> 00:42:46.400
We've had contact with them.

395
00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:49.399
- Contact? How?
- Radio.

396
00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:52.400
- They speak our language?
- Well not quite.

397
00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:55.279
We received messages
from their space ships.

398
00:42:55.379 --> 00:42:58.800
For a while it came in as
just a lot of jumbled noise.

399
00:42:58.900 --> 00:43:00.600
And now, Sir?

400
00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:04.180
Well since they first uh tried
contact with us by radio,

401
00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:06.500
...we've developed a language computer.

402
00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:10.400
A machine that breaks down
any language to our own.

403
00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:14.500
General, uh, what's this
all got to do with me?

404
00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:18.900
Well you've been in charge of saucer
field activity for a long while.

405
00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:22.800
I think it's about time you heard
these recordings. Do you mind?

406
00:43:22.900 --> 00:43:26.900
Mind? Huh, I'm anxious.

407
00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:38.115
This is Eros, a space soldier
from a planet of your galaxy.

408
00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:43.400
I fully realize our language differences,
however I also know you finally have...

409
00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:47.639
...perfected the dictorobitary,
or as you on Earth put it,

410
00:43:47.739 --> 00:43:52.600
...the language computer. So you can
now understand that which I speak.

411
00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:56.800
Since the beginning of your time, we
have been far beyond your planet.

412
00:43:56.900 --> 00:44:03.870
It has taken you centuries to even grasp
what we developed eons of your years ago.

413
00:44:04.100 --> 00:44:06.668
Do you still believe it
impossible we exist?

414
00:44:06.768 --> 00:44:11.300
You didn't actually think you were the
only inhabited planet in the universe?

415
00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.099
How can any race be so stupid?

416
00:44:14.100 --> 00:44:16.745
Permit me to set your mind at ease.

417
00:44:16.845 --> 00:44:20.800
We do not want to conquer
your planet. Only save it.

418
00:44:20.900 --> 00:44:25.900
We could have destroyed it long
ago, if that had been our aim.

419
00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:27.883
Our principal purpose is friendly.

420
00:44:27.983 --> 00:44:31.400
I admit, we have had to take
certain means which you might...

421
00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:33.699
...refer to as criminal,

422
00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:35.733
...but that is because of your big guns...

423
00:44:35.833 --> 00:44:38.400
...which have destroyed some
of our representatives.

424
00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:41.918
If you persist in denying us our landings,

425
00:44:42.018 --> 00:44:47.500
...then we must only accept that you
do not want us on friendly terms.

426
00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:53.300
We then have no alternative but to
destroy you before you destroy us.

427
00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:57.435
With your ancient, juvenile minds,
you have developed explosives...

428
00:44:57.535 --> 00:45:00.900
...too fast for your minds to
conceive what you were doing.

429
00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:05.100
You are on the verge of
destroying the entire universe.

430
00:45:05.200 --> 00:45:07.200
We are part of that universe.

431
00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:09.300
This is our last...

432
00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:15.277
That's the end of that one.

433
00:45:15.377 --> 00:45:19.400
Atmospheric conditions in outer space
often interfere with transmitting.

434
00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:21.900
How many of these recordings
do you have General?

435
00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.449
An even dozen up to now.
This was the last one.

436
00:45:24.549 --> 00:45:26.200
We received it over a month ago.

437
00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:29.600
- Do you think they mean business?
- We can't afford to take any chances.

438
00:45:30.100 --> 00:45:31.000
Come over here.

439
00:45:34.300 --> 00:45:36.100
You ever been to Hollywood?

440
00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:40.000
- Oh, a couple of times. A few years ago.
- You're going to be there in the morning.

441
00:45:40.100 --> 00:45:42.967
Just a few minutes from Hollywood,
in the...

442
00:45:43.067 --> 00:45:46.000
...town of San Fernando,
reports have come in...

443
00:45:46.100 --> 00:45:50.800
...of saucers flying so low the
exhaust knocked people to the ground.

444
00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:55.300
There have even been stated
claims of saucer landings.

445
00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:59.000
Major Carlson will replace
you while you're out there.

446
00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:02.300
You're the best man for the job
of attempting to contact them.

447
00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:06.100
Find them, Colonel. See what
in hell it is they want.

448
00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:08.200
All right, Sir.

449
00:46:13.100 --> 00:46:16.600
These are confidential reports, Colonel.
Read them over carefully on the plane,

450
00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:19.915
...turn them over to intelligence
when you get to Los Angeles.

451
00:46:20.015 --> 00:46:22.400
They'll have further
orders for disposition.

452
00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:24.500
Yes sir.

453
00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:28.699
- Colonel Edwards?
- Yes sir?

454
00:46:28.700 --> 00:46:30.910
- Good luck.
- Thank you, Sir.

455
00:46:59.800 --> 00:47:01.600
We are ready to report, Excellency.

456
00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:05.699
- You are many days late.
- It was unavoidable.

457
00:47:05.700 --> 00:47:08.465
We tried to transmit via
televisor, but atmospheric...

458
00:47:08.565 --> 00:47:10.600
...conditions made
transmission impossible.

459
00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:13.650
You should have transmitted as
soon as conditions permitted.

460
00:47:13.750 --> 00:47:15.400
I thought time was of the essence.

461
00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:17.533
Suspicion has fallen upon our movements.

462
00:47:17.633 --> 00:47:20.500
Our ships have been viewed
near the point of operations.

463
00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:23.600
And what has this added time
gained you, Eros?

464
00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:27.600
We have successfully risen
three of the dead ones.

465
00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:30.700
- Permit me to see one.
- Bring in the big one.

466
00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:34.800
Use your small electrode gun.

467
00:47:37.700 --> 00:47:39.900
I have taken two ships from your command.

468
00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:43.499
But... that will leave only my ship.

469
00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:47.400
It is necessary that you
continue your mission alone.

470
00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:51.399
I have need of your other ships elsewhere.

471
00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:55.160
Even though you have risen
three of the Earth dead,

472
00:47:55.260 --> 00:47:57.600
...the plan is far from successful,

473
00:47:57.700 --> 00:48:00.600
...and you Eros, must prove it an
operational success before...

474
00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:06.700
...more time, energy, ships and your
countrymen may be spent on it.

475
00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:10.900
We will not fail.
Everything is on our side.

476
00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:15.505
Not everything.
You do not have the live Earth people.

477
00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:24.700
You report that your ship was viewed
at scene of your present operations?

478
00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.800
That is correct.

479
00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:31.300
They have been viewed many times,
but not at the scene of operations.

480
00:48:31.400 --> 00:48:34.290
Something must be done about that.

481
00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:44.700
Stop him Tanna. He's close enough.
Turn off your electrode gun.

482
00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:48.800
No. No. Stop him Tanna.

483
00:48:49.700 --> 00:48:52.200
- I can't get it, it's jammed.
- Stop him you fool.

484
00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:57.570
Drop the gun to the floor, Tanna.
The metal will break contact.

485
00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:05.299
That was too close.

486
00:49:05.300 --> 00:49:07.300
Yes.

487
00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:15.845
Bring the giant here that
I may get a better look at him.

488
00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:32.100
Yes, he's a fine specimen.
Are they all this powerful on planet Earth?

489
00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:34.900
This one is an exception, Excellency.

490
00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:39.700
- What are the other two like?
- One is a woman, the other an old man.

491
00:49:39.800 --> 00:49:43.000
- An old man, you say?
- Yes, Excellency.

492
00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:50.699
This gives me a plan. Put the big one away.

493
00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:52.175
Pick up your electrode gun.

494
00:49:52.275 --> 00:49:55.500
Make sure it's in working order
before pointing it at him.

495
00:49:55.600 --> 00:49:57.800
Whatever made it jam must have
been cleared by the fall.

496
00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:00.110
Take him back to the ship.

497
00:50:11.100 --> 00:50:15.779
The old one must be
sacrificed. Re-land on Earth.

498
00:50:15.879 --> 00:50:19.400
Send the old one to enter a dwelling.

499
00:50:20.600 --> 00:50:25.600
Then cut the electrokinetic and
turn on your ship's decomposure ray.

500
00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:30.888
The result will astound those watching.
Astound them enough to delay their...

501
00:50:30.988 --> 00:50:35.700
...intention until you have gained
your other recruits from the cemetery.

502
00:50:35.800 --> 00:50:39.099
Yes, Excellency. It'll be done.

503
00:50:39.100 --> 00:50:42.925
Report to me when this
has been accomplished.

504
00:50:49.400 --> 00:50:53.500
Eros, the Earth people are
getting closer to that which we fear.

505
00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:57.200
Since they will not listen
or respect our existence,

506
00:50:57.300 --> 00:50:59.396
...they cannot help but
believe our powers when...

507
00:50:59.496 --> 00:51:01.500
...they see their own dead
walking 'round again,

508
00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:04.250
...brought about by our
advancement in such things.

509
00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:07.000
As soon as you have enough
of the dead recruits,

510
00:51:07.100 --> 00:51:11.102
...march them on the capitals of the Earth,
let nothing stand in your way. Their own...

511
00:51:11.202 --> 00:51:16.087
...dead will be used to make them accept
our existence, and believe in that fact.

512
00:51:59.200 --> 00:52:03.100
Mr. and Mrs. Trent... this is
Colonel Edwards from Washington DC.

513
00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:05.500
- Good evening, Colonel.
- Hello Colonel.

514
00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:07.700
Colonel would like to
ask you a few questions.

515
00:52:07.800 --> 00:52:12.220
- Questions? What about, Colonel?
- May I, uh, sit down?

516
00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:14.699
Oh, I'm sorry, please do.

517
00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:16.813
I want to ask you about
your strange experience...

518
00:52:16.913 --> 00:52:18.800
...the other night, when
you saw the flying saucer.

519
00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:25.800
After that the police brought me home.

520
00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:29.299
I hope I never see such a sight again.

521
00:52:29.300 --> 00:52:32.600
Well after your description I don't
think I'd want to see it either.

522
00:52:32.700 --> 00:52:33.700
One thing more,

523
00:52:33.800 --> 00:52:36.800
...after you were forced to
the ground by that blast of wind,

524
00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:42.499
- ...was it a hot or cold blast?
- It's kind of hard to explain.

525
00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:46.002
It wasn't hot, wasn't cold,
it was just a terrific...

526
00:52:46.102 --> 00:52:48.800
...force.
We-we couldn't get off the ground.

527
00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:52.800
The light blinded me so
badly I couldn't see a thing.

528
00:52:52.900 --> 00:52:57.099
We could only feel the pressure
of the wind, until it was gone.

529
00:52:57.199 --> 00:52:58.700
When the glare left us,

530
00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:01.700
...we could see a glowing ball
disappearing off in the distance.

531
00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:04.435
- Which way?
- Toward the cemetery.

532
00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:23.700
This is the most fantastic
story I've ever heard.

533
00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:25.600
And every word of it's true, too.

534
00:53:25.700 --> 00:53:28.000
That's the fantastic part of it.

535
00:53:28.100 --> 00:53:32.300
We found a lot of suspicious
things out in that cemetery.

536
00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:37.840
Then again, didn't find anything
to base a fact or suspicion on.

537
00:53:37.900 --> 00:53:41.900
Hey, do you hear anything?

538
00:53:47.800 --> 00:53:50.200
You see anything out there Kelton?

539
00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:54.099
Too dark, Lieutenant.

540
00:53:54.100 --> 00:53:58.100
But something's started stinking awful bad.

541
00:54:04.600 --> 00:54:08.600
There's something out there.

542
00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:37.642
What do you make of that?

543
00:55:37.742 --> 00:55:41.400
You got me. It didn't look
that way a minute ago.

544
00:55:41.500 --> 00:55:43.175
What about your man?

545
00:55:43.275 --> 00:55:47.365
Oh, in the excitement I
forgot all about Kelton.

546
00:55:52.600 --> 00:55:56.000
Oh, he'll be all right in a few minutes.

547
00:55:56.800 --> 00:55:59.800
- Did you see that thing? Did you get it?
- We got it.

548
00:55:59.900 --> 00:56:03.700
What was it? It didn't fall,
I fired every bullet I had.

549
00:56:03.800 --> 00:56:09.300
So did I. I don't know what it was or what
happened, but unless that bag of bones over there...

550
00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:13.990
...can reassemble itself,
it's out of the running now.

551
00:56:24.100 --> 00:56:27.600
Colonel I've been out here so often you'd
think I'd taken a lease on this place.

552
00:56:27.700 --> 00:56:29.600
Not a long lease, I hope.

553
00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:34.700
I see what you mean. But you know, I can't
help but feel the answer's out here somewhere.

554
00:56:34.800 --> 00:56:36.800
Is the... girl safe?

555
00:56:37.700 --> 00:56:43.500
- Mrs. Trent you'd better stay with the car.
- Stay here alone? Not on your life.

556
00:56:43.600 --> 00:56:44.600
Modern women...

557
00:56:44.700 --> 00:56:46.600
Yeah, they been that way
all down through the ages.

558
00:56:46.700 --> 00:56:49.399
Especially in a spot like this.

559
00:56:49.400 --> 00:56:51.400
- Kelton. - Yes sir?
- Stay with Mrs. Trent.

560
00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:53.100
All right, Lieutenant.

561
00:56:53.200 --> 00:56:58.810
- Now you stay close to the officer, Honey.
- I'd feel safer with you.

562
00:57:01.200 --> 00:57:03.726
Now the Lieutenant knows best.

563
00:57:03.826 --> 00:57:09.530
Oh I don't like it, but I guess
there isn't much I can do about it.

564
00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:15.999
- You have a gun?
- No.

565
00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:18.000
Know how to use one?

566
00:57:18.400 --> 00:57:21.545
After four years in the Marine Corps?

567
00:57:23.500 --> 00:57:24.700
- Here.
- You think we'll need these?

568
00:57:24.800 --> 00:57:27.860
You can never tell. Let's get going.

569
00:57:46.100 --> 00:57:48.438
What do you expect to find out here?

570
00:57:48.538 --> 00:57:51.300
Well there's only one
answer to that Mr. Trent,

571
00:57:51.400 --> 00:57:54.300
...we'll know when we find it.
Inspector Clay's grave is right over here.

572
00:57:54.400 --> 00:57:57.465
- Is that the one you told
me was broken into? - Yes.

573
00:58:16.100 --> 00:58:17.600
- This it?
- Yeah.

574
00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:20.300
Looks to me like someone had
broken out instead of in.

575
00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:23.947
I figured that, but that's
impossible.

576
00:58:24.547 --> 00:58:27.800
Look, Colonel, some
things just can't happen.

577
00:58:28.200 --> 00:58:31.653
Yeah well after that apparition that
was draped across Mr. Trent's...

578
00:58:31.753 --> 00:58:34.800
...patio, I would say we should
keep our minds open to anything.

579
00:58:34.900 --> 00:58:38.800
Look, Colonel, I'm a policeman.
I've got to deal in facts.

580
00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:44.286
But, I guess I'll have to go along
with you. You know I bet my...

581
00:58:44.386 --> 00:58:47.256
...badge right now we haven't
seen the last of those weirdies.

582
00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:55.300
- They'll discover our ship soon.
- You going to let them find us?

583
00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:59.700
It's the only way. These are the same
men who have been so close so often.

584
00:58:59.800 --> 00:59:02.600
They must be halted before they
can inform others about us.

585
00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:05.375
- But there were others in the car.
- They'll be taken too.

586
00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:11.477
Send the big one for
girl and the policeman.

587
00:59:11.677 --> 00:59:14.007
I'll turn on the dictorobitary
so we may converse with them.

588
00:59:45.600 --> 00:59:48.300
You know, maybe we're
barking up the wrong tree.

589
00:59:48.400 --> 00:59:51.300
One thing a policeman learns,
Mr. Trent, is patience.

590
00:59:51.400 --> 00:59:56.245
- Where's that burn spot you mentioned.
- Right over the... Look!

591
01:00:06.100 --> 01:00:09.330
We'll investigate, but move carefully.

592
01:00:52.500 --> 01:00:57.400
A moment or two more, and you will
be the first live Earth people...

593
01:00:57.500 --> 01:01:00.390
...ever to enter a celestial ship.

594
01:01:00.900 --> 01:01:06.255
Wow... boy, how could anything
that big hide for so long a time?

595
01:01:07.400 --> 01:01:10.100
Never heard metal sound like that before.

596
01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:11.200
What do you see?

597
01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:14.800
Only my reflection. Must be
some kind of one-way glass.

598
01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:20.340
- I wonder how you get into this thing.
- I'm not sure I want to find out.

599
01:01:31.500 --> 01:01:33.500
They're just outside.

600
01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:41.505
You can open the outer hatch now.

601
01:01:56.800 --> 01:01:59.800
- You goin' in that thing?
- That's what we're here for.

602
01:01:59.900 --> 01:02:02.478
I don't know, the way these
things speed around...

603
01:02:02.578 --> 01:02:05.050
...we might just get in
there and pft. Off it goes.

604
01:02:05.150 --> 01:02:06.400
That's a chance we take.

605
01:02:06.500 --> 01:02:09.259
Well, I took a chance on those earlier
airplanes. Might just as well...

606
01:02:09.359 --> 01:02:12.000
...see what the inside of one of
these looks like. Got your guns ready?

607
01:02:12.100 --> 01:02:15.420
I tell you one thing, if a
little green man pops out on me...

608
01:02:15.422 --> 01:02:18.497
...I'm shooting first
and asking questions later.

609
01:02:27.100 --> 01:02:29.905
They're in the outer chamber now.

610
01:02:30.600 --> 01:02:34.600
Eros, do we have to kill them?

611
01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:40.000
- Yes.
- It seems such a waste.

612
01:02:40.600 --> 01:02:45.742
Well wouldn't it be better to kill a few
now than, with their meddling, permit...

613
01:02:45.842 --> 01:02:48.100
...them to destroy the entire universe?

614
01:02:48.200 --> 01:02:50.800
- You're always right, Eros.
- Of course.

615
01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:56.085
But those are not my words,
those are the words of the Ruler.

616
01:03:01.700 --> 01:03:05.015
Now you two stay right where you're at.

617
01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:12.257
We will do as you command.
For the moment.

618
01:03:12.357 --> 01:03:16.947
No for the moment about it.
You just do as I tell you.

619
01:03:23.500 --> 01:03:27.399
- You do not need guns.
- Maybe we think we do.

620
01:03:27.400 --> 01:03:29.339
They would be of no use to you now.

621
01:03:29.439 --> 01:03:32.400
They've been mighty useful
before on flesh and blood,

622
01:03:32.500 --> 01:03:36.495
...and you two look like
you've got a lot of both.

623
01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:40.798
True, they would be
effective upon us. If you...

624
01:03:40.898 --> 01:03:43.400
...were to have the
opportunity to use them...

625
01:03:43.500 --> 01:03:47.466
Mister, if you don't get away from that
control board I'll show you just how...

626
01:03:47.566 --> 01:03:49.491
...effective they can be.

627
01:03:52.300 --> 01:03:56.400
Shall we talk now, or wait?
Your friends will be here shortly.

628
01:03:56.500 --> 01:04:00.240
- What friends?
- Those you left at the vehicle.

629
01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:03.800
- If you've done anything to Paula...
- Take it easy Mr. Trent.

630
01:04:03.900 --> 01:04:09.340
Oh I assure you, no harm has come
to her. Would you like to see?

631
01:04:10.400 --> 01:04:12.935
Next time you try that I
won't aim at the board.

632
01:04:13.035 --> 01:04:14.600
You're a headstrong young man.

633
01:04:14.700 --> 01:04:20.905
I was only going to turn on the
televisor so you could see her movements.

634
01:04:21.700 --> 01:04:25.700
Go ahead, my friend,
but move very carefully.

635
01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:42.200
She's only fainted.

636
01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:48.700
- You fiend.
- I? A fiend?

637
01:04:48.705 --> 01:04:51.400
I am a soldier of our planet.

638
01:04:52.100 --> 01:04:57.900
I? A fiend? We did not
come here as enemies.

639
01:04:58.800 --> 01:05:02.600
We came only with friendly
intentions. To talk.

640
01:05:02.700 --> 01:05:04.200
To ask your aid.

641
01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:06.899
- Our aid?
- Yes.

642
01:05:06.900 --> 01:05:10.528
Your aid for the whole universe.
But your governments...

643
01:05:10.628 --> 01:05:13.600
...of Earth refused even
to accept our existence.

644
01:05:13.700 --> 01:05:20.300
Even though you've seen us, heard our
messages, you still refused to accept us.

645
01:05:20.400 --> 01:05:24.200
Why is it so important that you want to
contact the governments of our Earth?

646
01:05:24.300 --> 01:05:28.890
Because of death. Because
all you of Earth are idiots.

647
01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:33.500
- Now you just hold on, Buster.
- No you hold on.

648
01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:38.900
First was your firecracker, a harmless
explosive. Then your hand grenade.

649
01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:46.000
They began to kill your own people a few at
a time. Then the bomb, then a larger bomb.

650
01:05:46.100 --> 01:05:50.900
Many people are killed at one
time. Then your scientists...

651
01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:55.000
...stumbled upon the atom
bomb. Split the atom.

652
01:05:55.400 --> 01:06:01.010
Then the hydrogen bomb, where you
actually explode the air itself.

653
01:06:01.100 --> 01:06:04.605
Now...
brings the destruction...

654
01:06:04.705 --> 01:06:07.900
...of the entire universe,
served by our sun.

655
01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:12.000
The only explosion left is the solaronite.

656
01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:15.900
- Why there's no such thing.
- Perhaps to you.

657
01:06:16.000 --> 01:06:19.194
But we've known it for
centuries. Your scientists...

658
01:06:19.294 --> 01:06:22.300
...will stumble upon it as
they have all the others.

659
01:06:22.400 --> 01:06:29.100
But the juvenile minds which you possess will
not comprehend its strength, until it's too late.

660
01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:32.199
You're way above our heads.

661
01:06:32.200 --> 01:06:36.700
The solaronite is a way to explode
the actual particles of sunlight.

662
01:06:36.800 --> 01:06:39.099
Why that's impossible.

663
01:06:39.100 --> 01:06:43.400
Even now, your scientists are working
on a way to harness the sun's rays.

664
01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:46.300
The rays of sunlight are minute particles.

665
01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:50.300
Is it so far from your imagination
they cannot do as I have suggested?

666
01:06:50.400 --> 01:06:53.600
Why a particle of sunlight
can't even be seen or measured.

667
01:06:53.700 --> 01:06:58.460
Can you see or measure an atom?
Yet you can explode one.

668
01:06:58.700 --> 01:07:01.500
A ray of sunlight is made up many atoms.

669
01:07:01.600 --> 01:07:03.726
So what if we do develop this solaronite...

670
01:07:03.826 --> 01:07:06.000
...bomb? We'd be even a
stronger nation than now.

671
01:07:06.100 --> 01:07:12.100
Stronger. You see? You see? Your
stupid minds... stupid. Stupid.

672
01:07:12.300 --> 01:07:16.000
- That's all I'm taking from you.
- Get back here you jerk.

673
01:07:16.100 --> 01:07:18.100
Let him finish.

674
01:07:25.100 --> 01:07:28.300
It's because of men like you
that all must be destroyed.

675
01:07:28.400 --> 01:07:33.400
Headstrong, violent.
No use of the mind God gave you.

676
01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:36.899
You talk of God?

677
01:07:36.900 --> 01:07:42.170
You also think it impossible that
we, too, might think of God?

678
01:07:43.900 --> 01:07:51.125
You, who wear the uniform of your country.
You see, I wear the uniform of my country.

679
01:07:52.000 --> 01:07:58.500
Yes, we've had to use drastic means to get
to you, but you left us no alternative.

680
01:07:58.600 --> 01:08:05.600
When you have the solaronite, you
have nothing. Nor, does the universe.

681
01:08:06.900 --> 01:08:10.900
You speak of solaronite,
but just what is it?

682
01:08:11.800 --> 01:08:17.800
Take a can of your gasoline. Say
this can of gasoline is the sun.

683
01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:24.865
Now you spread a thin a line of it
to a ball, representing the Earth.

684
01:08:26.000 --> 01:08:31.000
Now, the gasoline represents
the sunlight, the sun particles.

685
01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:36.030
Here we saturate the ball with
the gasoline, the sunlight.

686
01:08:36.400 --> 01:08:41.200
Then we put a flame to the ball. The flame
will speedily travel around the Earth,

687
01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:43.900
...back along the line of gasoline to can,

688
01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:49.042
...or the sun itself. It will
explode this source, and spread...

689
01:08:49.142 --> 01:08:53.732
...to every place that
gasoline, or sunlight, touches.

690
01:08:55.000 --> 01:09:00.695
Explode the sunlight here, Gentlemen,
and you explode the universe.

691
01:09:00.700 --> 01:09:06.900
Explode the sunlight here, and a chain
reaction will occur, direct to the sun itself.

692
01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:10.999
And to all the planets
that sunlight touches.

693
01:09:11.000 --> 01:09:16.270
To every planet in the universe.
This why you must be stopped.

694
01:09:17.400 --> 01:09:21.400
This is why any means
must be used to stop you.

695
01:09:21.700 --> 01:09:26.700
In a friendly manner,
or as it seems, you want it.

696
01:09:27.800 --> 01:09:28.800
He's mad.

697
01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:33.500
Mad? Is it mad that you destroy
other people to save yourselves?

698
01:09:33.600 --> 01:09:35.100
You have done this.

699
01:09:35.200 --> 01:09:39.100
Is it mad that one country must
destroy another to save themselves?

700
01:09:39.200 --> 01:09:43.400
You have also done this.
How then is it mad that one planet...

701
01:09:43.500 --> 01:09:48.448
...must destroy another that
threatens the very existence...

702
01:09:48.548 --> 01:09:49.800
That's enough.

703
01:09:49.900 --> 01:09:54.100
In my land, women are for advancing
the race, not for fighting...

704
01:09:54.200 --> 01:09:59.200
...man's battles. Life is not
so expansive on my planet.

705
01:09:59.500 --> 01:10:06.810
We don't cling to it like you do. Our entire
aim is for the development of our planet.

706
01:10:23.500 --> 01:10:25.600
- What happened to you?
- How come you're all alone?

707
01:10:25.700 --> 01:10:27.063
I asked for lots of help.

708
01:10:27.163 --> 01:10:29.600
You sounded drunk or
something on the radio.

709
01:10:29.700 --> 01:10:32.600
If I didn't see it with my own eyes
I would never have believed it.

710
01:10:32.700 --> 01:10:33.700
Believed what?

711
01:10:33.800 --> 01:10:36.100
It was horrible. And he
almost broke my shoulder.

712
01:10:36.200 --> 01:10:39.024
Look, what are you trying to
say? If you don't make sense...

713
01:10:39.124 --> 01:10:41.900
...we'll never get to the bottom
of this. Now who slugged you?

714
01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:43.600
Inspector Clay.

715
01:10:43.700 --> 01:10:44.700
What?

716
01:10:44.800 --> 01:10:48.900
It was Clay all right, only
not like we remembered him.

717
01:10:49.000 --> 01:10:51.700
Well his grave was busted into, wasn't it?

718
01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:53.900
Next you'll tell me you saw skeletons.

719
01:10:54.000 --> 01:10:58.100
- We did, earlier.
- Now I know you're off your rocker.

720
01:10:58.200 --> 01:11:01.400
All of us saw, the Lieutenant,
the Colonel, everybody.

721
01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:04.200
- Where's the Lieutenant now?
- We've gotta find them.

722
01:11:04.300 --> 01:11:07.022
Mrs. Trent is gone.
I was left here to guard her.

723
01:11:07.122 --> 01:11:09.900
Then Clay showed up and
put me out of the running.

724
01:11:10.000 --> 01:11:12.800
And the second time tonight and
I'm getting darned tired of it.

725
01:11:12.900 --> 01:11:15.200
- Which way were they going?
- Oh, that way.

726
01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:17.300
Come on.

727
01:11:23.600 --> 01:11:28.500
Then one day it could all be
gone, in one big puff of smoke...

728
01:11:28.600 --> 01:11:32.600
...and ball of fire. All that out there,

729
01:11:33.400 --> 01:11:36.899
...the stars, the planets,

730
01:11:36.900 --> 01:11:39.999
...all just an empty void.

731
01:11:40.000 --> 01:11:42.600
You two had better come along with us.

732
01:11:42.700 --> 01:11:46.700
- Come with you? Where?
- The police station.

733
01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:53.200
So it seems you think
you have the upper hand.

734
01:11:53.500 --> 01:11:55.500
Look out there.

735
01:12:00.600 --> 01:12:02.600
Jeff.

736
01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:17.485
She is unharmed, but he would
kill in seconds if I so choose.

737
01:12:26.300 --> 01:12:28.300
Holy cow.

738
01:12:35.400 --> 01:12:37.400
Look there.

739
01:12:40.800 --> 01:12:45.400
- It's Clay all right, there's no mistaking that.
- And he's got Mrs. Trent.

740
01:12:45.500 --> 01:12:46.551
Get your gun ready.

741
01:12:46.651 --> 01:12:49.500
From all I've seen tonight
guns won't do any good.

742
01:12:49.600 --> 01:12:52.083
Clay is dead, and we
buried him. How are we...

743
01:12:52.183 --> 01:12:54.500
...going to kill somebody
that's already dead?

744
01:12:54.600 --> 01:12:59.600
Dead. And yet there he stands. That other
one earlier I emptied a full clip into him.

745
01:12:59.700 --> 01:13:04.715
I'm seeing it, that's the only
reason I'm listening to you.

746
01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:11.000
Look, I've got an idea. Hurt him or
not, we've got to try something.

747
01:13:11.100 --> 01:13:13.275
I'm going to sneak up
behind him and whop...

748
01:13:13.375 --> 01:13:15.600
...him over the head. That
oughta make him move.

749
01:13:15.700 --> 01:13:19.900
Follow me. Even when Clay was alive he
couldn't run fast enough to catch me,

750
01:13:20.000 --> 01:13:22.353
...so when he does, you grab
Mrs. Trent and run...

751
01:13:22.453 --> 01:13:24.500
...like lightning
in the opposite direction.

752
01:13:24.600 --> 01:13:29.190
- Oh, you think it will work?
- Know anything else to try?

753
01:13:44.700 --> 01:13:49.700
Oh, I'll be all right.
Take care of the others.

754
01:13:50.600 --> 01:13:54.299
Your men have felled the big one.

755
01:13:54.300 --> 01:13:57.900
This could only happen because
the electrode ray is off.

756
01:13:58.000 --> 01:13:59.900
He'll walk again when I turn it on.

757
01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:01.600
Hold it, right there.

758
01:14:02.700 --> 01:14:04.453
Suppose the Lieutenant and
the others are in that thing.

759
01:14:04.553 --> 01:14:08.143
Well suppose there are martians
or something in there.

760
01:14:16.100 --> 01:14:18.100
Come on, let's go.

761
01:14:20.100 --> 01:14:22.310
Open up in there, open up.

762
01:14:26.700 --> 01:14:28.289
Get that door open.

763
01:14:28.389 --> 01:14:30.280
Colonel I wouldn't know
one switch from another.

764
01:14:45.800 --> 01:14:50.560
Hold on Eros, I'll have the
ship in the air in a minute.

765
01:15:10.200 --> 01:15:13.770
Get out of here, Jeff. The ship's on fire.

766
01:15:32.900 --> 01:15:34.999
Eros.

767
01:15:35.000 --> 01:15:41.545
Eros everything's on fire. Hurry, wake
up Eros. Eros, Eros, wake up, wake up.

768
01:15:46.100 --> 01:15:50.100
Eros. Eros wake up, Eros. God wake up.

769
01:16:09.400 --> 01:16:11.400
Oh, I wonder if that's the
last we'll see of them?

770
01:16:11.500 --> 01:16:14.300
Perhaps, but sooner or
later there'll be others.

771
01:16:14.400 --> 01:16:18.400
Look.

772
01:16:20.500 --> 01:16:24.100
Have they caught that
woman, that thing yet?

773
01:16:24.200 --> 01:16:26.600
Hey that's right, there's
another ghoul running loose.

774
01:16:26.700 --> 01:16:29.000
And it's my guess
that she'll look like him.

775
01:16:29.100 --> 01:16:33.945
With the ship and the ray gun
gone, they have no control.

776
01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:39.100
We got to hand it to them though,
they're far ahead of us.

777
01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:46.600
Fire. Eros.

778
01:16:53.800 --> 01:16:57.800
My friend, you have seen this
incident based on sworn testimony.

779
01:16:58.900 --> 01:17:00.800
Can you prove that it didn't happen?

780
01:17:01.900 --> 01:17:05.000
Perhaps on your way home,
someone will pass you in the dark,

781
01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:10.370
...and you will never know it, for
they will be from outer space.

782
01:17:11.800 --> 01:17:15.400
Many scientists believe that another
world is watching us this moment.

783
01:17:15.500 --> 01:17:18.300
We once laughed at the
horseless carriage,

784
01:17:18.400 --> 01:17:23.800
...the aeroplane, the telephone,
the electric light, vitamins,

785
01:17:23.900 --> 01:17:26.099
...radio, and even television.

786
01:17:26.100 --> 01:17:30.100
And now some of us laugh at outer space.

787
01:17:35.000 --> 01:17:38.000
God help us... in the future.
